Subject: [PATCH] GS GPU renderer: settings lowered when the card falls behind; GPU resets told, not fatal (TODO 202)

Patch 0087 fitted the Resolution and Multisampling to the card's memory; on the GTX 1080 the game still crashed at
7x8 with 4 samples (5-10 pictures a second, then a GPU reset by Windows and 0xC0000409 in NVIDIA's OpenGL driver).
Memory was not the cause: the card was too slow.

The GPU-time watch (Impl::watchGpuTime): at each presentation a GPU timestamp is queued beside the GPU's clock at
that moment; when it comes back, the distance is how far behind the GPU was (its backlog; the game's own slowness
leaves none). Behind by more than 150 ms over most of the last 16 presentations, or by more than 800 ms once (also
for a timestamp still out), the settings go one step down the ladder 0087 uses (8 samples to 4, the Resolution
down to 4, samples to 2 and none, the Resolution further) for the rest of the run, judged afresh 3 s later. The
step (Impl::rescaleAll) sends every buffer to local memory as a buffer pushed out of the cache is, and makes the
pictures, the composing textures and the multisampled copies again at the new scale (old pictures deleted once
no presentation shows them). The settings stay as chosen; one log line a step. PS2X_GPU_TIME_WATCH=0 turns it
off; PS2X_GPU_SLOW_TEST=<ms> or x<n> (tests) measures each backlog that much longer or n times as long;
PS2X_GPU_TIME_LOG=1 logs the backlog once a second. Measured on an RX 7900 XTX at Resolution 8 with 8 samples
(Amazon): a median backlog of 4 ms (27 at most), so no step there.

The batch cap (Impl::capBatch), the part that kept the GTX 1080 alive: on it, the reset came from ONE batch, not
from the frame rate - at Resolution 6 without samples its frames took ~40 ms of the GPU, then one batch of a busy
scene (hundreds of thousands of vertices over the same pixels) took 300 ms and a later one passed 2 s (reset at
any setting from 5x6 up; 14 minutes fine at Resolution 2). A batch now ends at 65536 vertices (as at any other
batch boundary) and goes to the GPU at once (glFlush), so no single piece of GPU work grows past what the card does
in well under a second. Checked there: Resolution 6 without samples, which reset after ~170 s, ran 14 minutes with
no GPU event. On the RX 7900 XTX it ends ~4.5 batches more a picture, the same frame rate.
PS2X_GPU_BATCH_VERTICES=<n>: the cap (0: none). With PS2X_GPU_STATS or PS2X_GPU_PROFILE, a piece of GPU work over
250 ms is logged as it is seen (pass and kind).

Robust contexts: raylib's window context is made with GLFW_CONTEXT_ROBUSTNESS = LOSE_CONTEXT_ON_RESET (a line
added to the fetched raylib source by CMakeLists.txt; PS2X_GL_ROBUST=0 at run time leaves it out), and the GS
renderer's context asks the same. A GPU reset is then told (glGetGraphicsResetStatus) instead of the driver
ending the program: the game logs it, shows a message saying what to lower (not in a hidden window) and ends
with exit code 202. A lost context cannot be had back (the window's own is lost too). Drivers without
WGL_ARB_create_context_robustness (AMD's) keep plain contexts, as before. PS2X_GPU_RESET_TEST=<VBlank> (tests):
as if the reset came then.


